The calling operation was built for coverage: reach every lead and let none slip. Then the volume outgrew the team's capacity to act on it, and coverage stopped being a strategy.

This is one piece of work in three chapters. First we learned who to call first. Then we proved which calls were not worth making. Then we designed a model to score every lead on its own. Each chapter has a figure to work through, and each figure says which parts are measured and which are drawn to show the idea.

## Chapter 1: who to call first

More leads arrived than the team could ever call. The question was no longer how to call more, but who to call first, and that is a question about intent, not volume.

I came at it from the marketing side, where the leads were generated, and worked across into how the calling ran. The lead form already asked two questions: when someone wanted to study, and how ready they were to enroll. That second question described the reason for enquiring through intent to enroll. I combined them into a matrix of every answer pairing and scored each pairing on how often leads like it had gone on to apply. That gave us **three intent tiers**. Try a pairing below.

It was a coarse cut, built on a thin slice of what we could have known. But it was the moment the operation started prioritising on intent instead of recency. What it could not tell us was which calls were not worth making at all.

## Chapter 2: who to stop calling

The tiers told us who to call first, but not who to stop calling, so we kept calling everyone. Then growth outran the process again: leads were up **8.1%** while call output fell **16%**. Before anyone argued to hire, I asked where the effort was already going.

I took seven months of CRM data and measured 30-day application rates by tier, on mature cohorts only. I split the period into three acts, so a dialler backlog in the middle of it could not pass for strategy. The answer is in the figure. Switch it from before to after.

The bottom tier was taking **27.8% of call effort** for almost nothing, while top-tier leads applied at **eight times the rate**. We stopped calling it. Effort there fell to **0.7%** and conversion held: the applications still arrived, on the leads' timeline rather than the dialler's.

The tiers had proved the principle, that intent predicts who converts. But two questions was a blunt way to measure it. That earned the mandate for something sharper.

## Chapter 3: a score for every lead

Stopping the calls we did not need was the proof. The model was the point. Three tiers can only cut one way: drop the whole bottom tier, or keep it. A **decile model** scores each lead on its own expected conversion and ranks the leads into ten equal bands, so the cutoff can sit wherever the evidence puts it.

I ran alignment sessions with six stakeholders across admissions, marketing, paid media and data engineering, and took the model to senior leaders, who endorsed it. Then the groundwork: I audited the signals we had, wrote the analysis query myself, **around 600k leads with point-in-time features**, and set an interpretability requirement so the team could trust the scores. Drag the cutoff below to see what the finer scale buys.

The bars in that figure are drawn, not measured, because the model has not launched. It is designed, documented end to end, and lands in the next couple of months. When it does, the share of call effort we can safely cut should climb from the **roughly 27%** the tiers reached toward **half**.

## What is proven, and what is not

Two of these three chapters are results. The tiers are in use, and the bottom-tier decision is measured: effort moved and conversion held. The third is a design with a dataset behind it. Half is an expectation until the model has run on live leads.

The scores are meant to guide more than calling. Nurture and media spend can follow the same evidence. The whole workstream is written to run without me as the dependency.
